Sequences for seqprobe.c and its counterpart on our side.

One operation per line, `#` for a comment, blank lines ignored. Both ends
must refuse an unknown word LOUDLY rather than skip it: a line one side
executes and the other ignores produces a diff that looks like a finding
about the editor and is not. seqprobe exits 4 when it did not understand
something and names the line.

    type <rest of line, spaces and all>
    key <enter|back|del|home|end|left|right|up|down|tab> [+ctrl] [+shift]
    sel <from> <to>
    bold <0|1>    ital <0|1>    under <0|1>
    face <name>   size <points>
    align <l|c|r>
    indent <start> <offset> <right>      twips
    bullet <0|1>
    copy | cut | paste
    undo | redo
    resize <w> <h>

The control is 400x200 with no EM_SETRECT, and that is part of the contract
rather than an implementation detail: wrapprobe.c measured that a control
with a formatting rectangle and one without wrap differently, so two sides
disagreeing about it would report a difference on every wrapping sequence.

These seven are alice's, and they are not arbitrary. Every bug jd found by
hand lived in an interaction between features rather than in one of them:
a marker clamp that only failed when another marker had moved first, a
toggle that only failed on the first of a run, an armed format that failed
to close a typed run. A sequence that only types cannot find any of them.
